WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:19.857
Oh! Hi there, Little Fox readers! I didn't see you for a second. I guess I was too busy working on my design.

2
00:00:19.858 --> 00:00:29.009
I'm going to enter it in a contest at the community playground. First prize for designing the best playground fort is one-hundred dollars!

3
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:37.763
And speaking of forts, this week in our landmarks lesson, we learned about a really famous one: the Kremlin in Moscow, Russia.

4
00:00:37.764 --> 00:00:47.138
Lots of Russian cities have a kremlin—the Russian word for fortress or fort—but the one in Moscow is so famous that most people just call it the Kremlin.

5
00:00:47.139 --> 00:00:52.684
The Kremlin is an enormous collection of buildings that's shaped kind of like a triangle.

6
00:00:52.685 --> 00:01:01.996
And with its amazing cathedrals, palaces, and tall, colorful towers, the Kremlin is often described as an "architectural wonder."

7
00:01:01.997 --> 00:01:08.416
When it was first built back in 1156, the Kremlin was just a small wooden fortress.

8
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:13.963
Over time more buildings were added to the site, especially during the fifteenth century.

9
00:01:13.964 --> 00:01:20.969
That was when a czar named Ivan the Great ruled Russia, and he wanted to show off his power by expanding the place.

10
00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:27.054
Later other Russian rulers rebuilt some of the structures and added even more buildings to the grounds.

11
00:01:27.055 --> 00:01:34.356
Since this expansion occurred over a few centuries, there's now a huge variety of architectural styles at the Kremlin.

12
00:01:34.357 --> 00:01:40.427
The entire complex is surrounded by thick brick walls that have twenty towers built into them.

13
00:01:40.451 --> 00:01:44.129
Each tower is unique with its own name.

14
00:01:44.129 --> 00:01:51.365
The oldest of these is the Secret Tower, named this because it used to contain a secret tunnel that led to the Moskva River.

15
00:01:51.366 --> 00:01:58.849
So even if the Kremlin had been completely surrounded by an enemy army, people could have still sneaked to the river to get water!

16
00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:07.541
At the very center of the Kremlin is Cathedral Square, a large open space surrounded by—you guessed it—cathedrals!

17
00:02:07.542 --> 00:02:12.692
In fact, many people think these buildings are among the most beautiful churches in the world.

18
00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:17.171
The Cathedral of the Assumption is the oldest and largest of these.

19
00:02:17.171 --> 00:02:22.582
Unfortunately, when the cathedral was almost complete, it was destroyed by an earthquake.

20
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:29.906
However, work on the cathedral was continued, and in 1479 it was finally finished.

21
00:02:29.907 --> 00:02:36.149
The Cathedral of the Assumption is a massive building topped with five golden onion domes.

22
00:02:36.173 --> 00:02:40.983
The domes are called this because they look like . . . well, onions, of course!

23
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:46.066
According to Mr. Fay, onion domes are very common in Russian architecture.

24
00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:53.153
Another cathedral at the square, the Cathedral of the Annunciation, was used as a private chapel for the czars.

25
00:02:53.154 --> 00:02:58.722
Except there was one czar who wasn't allowed to enter it—Ivan the Terrible.

26
00:02:58.723 --> 00:03:05.396
Back then, according to the Russian Orthodox Church, a person could be married only three times.

27
00:03:05.397 --> 00:03:12.530
Well, Ivan the Terrible got married four times, so he was no longer permitted inside the church.

28
00:03:12.531 --> 00:03:17.798
Guess what Ivan the Terrible did to solve the problem . . . He added a porch to the building!

29
00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:22.860
That way he could sit outside and still hear any religious services going on inside.

30
00:03:22.861 --> 00:03:30.041
Right across from the Cathedral of the Annunciation is the Ivan the Great Bell Tower, the tallest building in the Kremlin.

31
00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:35.357
The Czar Bell, the largest bell in the world, sits at the foot of this tower.

32
00:03:35.358 --> 00:03:44.946
Mr. Fay told us it weighs just over 181 metric tons, which made me wonder something: what does a bell that big sound like?

33
00:03:44.947 --> 00:03:49.400
Well, nobody knows because the Czar Bell has never been rung.

34
00:03:49.424 --> 00:03:55.084
It got too hot while it was being cast, and a big piece broke off.

35
00:03:55.084 --> 00:04:00.176
Today about two-thirds of the Kremlin is used for official government business.

36
00:04:00.177 --> 00:04:04.513
In fact, the Senate Building is the official home of the Russian president.

37
00:04:04.537 --> 00:04:08.415
But there are many buildings that are open to the public for touring.

38
00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:13.333
A popular spot is the Armory, which is one of the biggest museums in the world.

39
00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:25.585
It holds lots of priceless artifacts collected by Russian czars over the centuries—swords, armor, pistols, clothing, jewelry, carriages, even Ivan the Terrible's throne!

40
00:04:25.586 --> 00:04:33.966
Visitors to the Armory can also check out the State Diamond Fund, a collection of Russian gems that includes the largest sapphire in the world.

41
00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:38.053
The State Kremlin Palace is another building that's open to the public.

42
00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:43.557
Built in the 1960s, this building is the newest one inside the complex.

43
00:04:43.557 --> 00:04:51.715
In fact, Mr. Fay said that its plain, straight lines and tall, modern windows make it look out of place among the older buildings.

44
00:04:51.716 --> 00:04:57.206
It has a huge 6,000-seat auditorium, which is used today as a concert hall.

45
00:04:57.207 --> 00:05:03.029
If you ever go to the Kremlin, you'll also see another important world landmark: Red Square.

46
00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:11.109
This large public space sits just outside the Kremlin's entrance and it was once the site of battles and military parades.

47
00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:16.008
But now it's used mainly for things like rock concerts and fashion shows.

48
00:05:16.032 --> 00:05:19.189
I bet it's a cool place to see a concert!

49
00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:26.197
At one end of the square is St. Basil's Cathedral, which Ivan the Terrible commissioned to celebrate a military victory.

50
00:05:26.221 --> 00:05:30.923
This cathedral is known for its brightly colored onion domes.

51
00:05:30.923 --> 00:05:37.278
Each dome has a unique color pattern and texture, giving the cathedral an extremely distinct appearance.

52
00:05:37.279 --> 00:05:45.922
Supposedly Ivan the Terrible wanted to make sure that the architect who designed the cathedral never built anything more beautiful than St. Basil's.

53
00:05:45.923 --> 00:05:50.353
So when the cathedral was finished, Ivan had him blinded!

54
00:05:50.377 --> 00:05:56.935
Or so says a popular legend—Mr. Fay told us historians are sure the story isn't true.

55
00:05:56.935 --> 00:06:02.894
But just in case, I'm glad Ivan the Terrible isn't judging the contest for best playground fort!

56
00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:11.744
That reminds me . . . I have to get back to my design for the new playground fort. Wish me luck in the contest!
